WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.859
Shivaar, lesson
two: pointer three.

00:00:03.979 --> 00:00:07.724
Fragments, the DialogFragment
with a listener,

00:00:07.844 --> 00:00:09.713
and the manifest.

00:00:09.833 --> 00:00:12.785
Slides first, then
the whiteboard,

00:00:12.905 --> 00:00:15.498
then a real app on the emulator.

00:00:15.618 --> 00:00:18.115
Then four classic mistakes,

00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:21.836
and two TODO questions on
your Smart Pantry app.

00:00:22.992 --> 00:00:25.920
Slide three: what is a Fragment?

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.985
It's a piece of a screen,

00:00:28.105 --> 00:00:31.394
with its own layout and
its own Java class.

00:00:31.514 --> 00:00:33.382
It can't live alone.

00:00:33.502 --> 00:00:36.383
It always sits
inside an Activity,

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:40.248
and you can reuse the same
piece on different screens.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.617
Slide four compares them.

00:00:43.737 --> 00:00:48.058
An Activity is a whole screen
and calls setContentView.

00:00:48.178 --> 00:00:51.035
A Fragment needs
a host Activity,

00:00:51.155 --> 00:00:54.924
and it inflates its own
XML into a container.

00:00:55.044 --> 00:00:58.812
Page one hundred and nine of
the study guide lists the

00:00:58.932 --> 00:01:02.245
DialogFragment as one
kind of Fragment.

00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:06.583
Slide five: the
tethered life cycle.

00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:10.496
onAttach: the fragment
is joined to its host.

00:01:10.616 --> 00:01:13.593
onCreate: the object is made.

00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:18.010
onCreateView: it builds its
layout and returns a View.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:20.843
onResume: it is on screen.

00:01:20.963 --> 00:01:23.891
It can never move
further than its host.

00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:28.394
The study guide, page
one hundred and ten:

00:01:28.514 --> 00:01:32.931
onCreateView inflates the layout
with container and false,

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:34.929
and returns the View.

00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:37.978
Slide seven shows
the same lines.

00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.114
And note this sentence:

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:44.051
fragments don't need to be
registered in your manifest.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.585
Slide nine: fragment
transactions.

00:01:48.705 --> 00:01:51.514
The FragmentManager
is in charge.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:54.418
beginTransaction starts a batch.

00:01:54.538 --> 00:01:57.851
replace puts a new fragment
into the container.

00:01:57.971 --> 00:02:00.852
addToBackStack
lets Back undo it.

00:02:00.972 --> 00:02:02.941
And commit applies it.

00:02:03.061 --> 00:02:05.918
Without commit, nothing happens.

00:02:07.075 --> 00:02:08.942
Whiteboard.

00:02:09.392 --> 00:02:13.352
Step one: the host's
layout, activity_main.

00:02:13.802 --> 00:02:18.315
Then the empty FrameLayout,
with the id fragmentContainer.

00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:21.094
It's an empty frame on the wall.

00:02:21.544 --> 00:02:24.401
Height zero dp with weight one,

00:02:24.851 --> 00:02:27.660
so it takes all the
space that's left.

00:02:29.117 --> 00:02:33.413
Step two: MainActivity puts
the first fragment in.

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:38.424
super, then setContentView,
so the container exists.

00:02:38.874 --> 00:02:42.787
If savedInstanceState is
null, it's the first start,

00:02:43.237 --> 00:02:45.374
as in your CA test.

00:02:45.824 --> 00:02:49.953
Then getSupportFragmentManager
dot beginTransaction.

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:56.062
Replace the container with a
new HomeFragment. Then commit.

00:02:56.512 --> 00:03:01.241
After a rotation, Android puts
the fragment back by itself.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.413
Step three: HomeFragment.

00:03:05.863 --> 00:03:09.512
It extends Fragment, with
an empty constructor.

00:03:09.962 --> 00:03:13.106
onCreateView inflates
fragment_home,

00:03:13.556 --> 00:03:15.837
with container and false.

00:03:16.287 --> 00:03:18.424
Find the button on view,

00:03:18.874 --> 00:03:22.331
because a fragment has
no setContentView.

00:03:22.781 --> 00:03:27.582
On a tap: getParentFragmentManager,
beginTransaction,

00:03:28.032 --> 00:03:32.544
replace with ProfileFragment,
addToBackStack, commit.

00:03:32.994 --> 00:03:34.863
And return the view.

00:03:36.325 --> 00:03:38.582
Here it is on the emulator.

00:03:38.702 --> 00:03:42.495
HomeFragment, in green,
sits in the container.

00:03:42.615 --> 00:03:46.167
Go to Profile swaps
in ProfileFragment.

00:03:46.287 --> 00:03:50.152
Back brings Home back,
thanks to addToBackStack.

00:03:50.272 --> 00:03:55.793
The real log: onAttach, onCreate,
onCreateView, onResume.

00:03:55.913 --> 00:04:00.378
After Back, only onCreateView
and onResume run again:

00:04:00.498 --> 00:04:03.979
the view was rebuilt,
the object was kept.

00:04:05.117 --> 00:04:07.157
The DialogFragment.

00:04:07.277 --> 00:04:11.262
Slide four of Modular
Android UI and State.

00:04:11.382 --> 00:04:15.751
Three stages: inflate its
layout in onCreateView,

00:04:15.871 --> 00:04:18.752
listen for the
button, and dismiss.

00:04:18.872 --> 00:04:22.881
getDialog dot dismiss
must run on every path,

00:04:23.001 --> 00:04:25.857
or the dialog stays
stuck on screen.

00:04:26.992 --> 00:04:30.088
How does the answer get
back to the Activity?

00:04:30.208 --> 00:04:32.873
Slide six: four steps.

00:04:32.993 --> 00:04:35.850
The dialog defines an interface.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:38.491
The Activity implements it.

00:04:38.611 --> 00:04:40.652
The dialog calls it.

00:04:40.772 --> 00:04:43.364
The Activity uses the value.

00:04:43.484 --> 00:04:47.157
The dialog never needs to
know which Activity it is.

00:04:48.283 --> 00:04:52.748
Study guide Listing four
point ten, page sixty-seven:

00:04:52.868 --> 00:04:55.029
the date picker dialog.

00:04:55.149 --> 00:05:00.310
SaveDateListener interface,
empty constructor, and saveItem:

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.142
call the listener, then dismiss.

00:05:04.283 --> 00:05:07.140
Our version: QuantityDialog.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.327
It extends DialogFragment.

00:05:10.777 --> 00:05:14.786
Inside it, the interface
SaveQuantityListener,

00:05:15.236 --> 00:05:19.940
with one method,
didFinishQuantityDialog, taking an int.

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:24.303
A field to hold the listener,
and the empty constructor.

00:05:24.753 --> 00:05:28.210
In onAttach, the
context is the host.

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.469
Cast it to the
listener and keep it.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:38.269
Study guide page one hundred and
eleven puts this in onAttach.

00:05:38.719 --> 00:05:44.897
Its listing casts getActivity
in saveItem. Both work.

00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:50.735
onCreateView inflates
dialog_quantity, with false,

00:05:51.185 --> 00:05:54.954
and finds the EditText and
the Save button on view.

00:05:55.404 --> 00:05:57.973
On Save, read the text.

00:05:58.423 --> 00:06:01.016
An empty box counts as zero.

00:06:01.466 --> 00:06:03.507
Then call saveItem.

00:06:03.957 --> 00:06:07.245
saveItem calls the host
through the listener,

00:06:07.695 --> 00:06:10.360
then getDialog dot dismiss.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:12.971
Cancel dismisses too.

00:06:13.421 --> 00:06:16.134
Every path closes the dialog.

00:06:17.617 --> 00:06:19.478
The host side.

00:06:19.928 --> 00:06:26.475
MainActivity implements QuantityDialog
dot SaveQuantityListener.

00:06:26.925 --> 00:06:28.795
That's the promise.

00:06:29.245 --> 00:06:33.206
On the button: new
QuantityDialog, then show,

00:06:33.656 --> 00:06:37.089
with getSupportFragme
ntManager and a tag.

00:06:37.539 --> 00:06:40.179
That's Listing
four point eleven.

00:06:40.629 --> 00:06:47.002
And didFinishQuantityDialog puts
the number on screen, as text.

00:06:48.492 --> 00:06:51.756
One more way, labelled
general Android,

00:06:52.206 --> 00:06:54.607
not the study guide's listing.

00:06:55.057 --> 00:06:57.338
Instead of onCreateView,

00:06:57.788 --> 00:07:04.810
override onCreateDialog and return
an AlertDialog dot Builder.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.077
Title, message, and
setPositiveButton,

00:07:09.527 --> 00:07:12.864
whose click calls the
host's didConfirmClear.

00:07:13.314 --> 00:07:16.675
These buttons close
the dialog themselves.

00:07:18.158 --> 00:07:22.719
This is the real project file,
in Android Studio's folders.

00:07:22.839 --> 00:07:25.024
Every line has a comment,

00:07:25.144 --> 00:07:28.217
so you can revise from
it on your laptop.

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:33.543
On the emulator: Set
quantity opens our dialog.

00:07:33.663 --> 00:07:36.160
I type four and tap Save.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:40.673
The Activity shows Quantity
four, and the dialog is gone.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:44.082
Clear quantity opens
the AlertDialog.

00:07:44.202 --> 00:07:46.771
Clear, and it's back to zero.

00:07:47.908 --> 00:07:49.778
The manifest.

00:07:49.898 --> 00:07:54.195
Slide four of Android App
Blueprints: it's the guest list.

00:07:54.315 --> 00:07:58.275
Every Activity must be
listed inside application.

00:07:58.395 --> 00:08:02.164
If one is missing, the app
crashes when you open it.

00:08:02.284 --> 00:08:05.429
Fragments and dialogs
are not listed.

00:08:06.575 --> 00:08:08.443
The front door.

00:08:08.563 --> 00:08:12.812
The study guide, page thirty-
three, and slide five.

00:08:12.932 --> 00:08:18.687
The intent-filter tells the OS
what to do with this Activity.

00:08:18.807 --> 00:08:21.664
Action MAIN: it's
the first screen.

00:08:21.784 --> 00:08:24.833
Category LAUNCHER:
give it an icon.

00:08:24.953 --> 00:08:28.242
Exported true lets the
launcher start it.

00:08:28.362 --> 00:08:32.826
That's required on Android
twelve and up: general Android.

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.399
Slide eight: versions.

00:08:36.519 --> 00:08:40.528
versionCode is an integer
that goes up every release.

00:08:40.648 --> 00:08:42.928
The Play Store compares it.

00:08:43.048 --> 00:08:46.289
versionName is the
text the user sees.

00:08:46.409 --> 00:08:49.386
The study guide,
page thirty-four,

00:08:49.506 --> 00:08:52.099
puts both in build dot gradle.

00:08:52.219 --> 00:08:57.092
Permissions, like INTERNET, go
in the manifest: slide nine.

00:08:58.242 --> 00:09:00.978
The manifest, line by line.

00:09:01.428 --> 00:09:04.813
uses-permission
INTERNET, an example.

00:09:05.263 --> 00:09:09.584
application, with the label
under the icon, and the theme.

00:09:10.034 --> 00:09:13.227
activity, name dot MainActivity.

00:09:13.677 --> 00:09:18.588
The dot means this app's
package. Exported true.

00:09:19.038 --> 00:09:23.311
The intent-filter: action
MAIN, category LAUNCHER.

00:09:23.761 --> 00:09:25.945
And no fragments anywhere.

00:09:27.408 --> 00:09:32.185
And build dot gradle, module
app, inside defaultConfig.

00:09:32.635 --> 00:09:35.420
versionCode two, an integer.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.087
versionName one
point one, a string.

00:09:40.575 --> 00:09:42.832
Gradle builds it for real.

00:09:42.952 --> 00:09:46.745
adb installs it, and
starts MainActivity.

00:09:46.865 --> 00:09:51.065
dumpsys reads the installed
app: versionCode two,

00:09:51.185 --> 00:09:55.530
versionName one point one,
straight from build dot gradle.

00:09:56.658 --> 00:10:00.571
Mistake one: the launcher
intent-filter is missing.

00:10:00.691 --> 00:10:03.260
It builds, and it installs.

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:07.005
But the app drawer has no
Pantry Fragments icon.

00:10:07.125 --> 00:10:11.206
The launcher test says: no
activities found to run.

00:10:12.367 --> 00:10:17.912
The fix: put MAIN and LAUNCHER
back. Rebuild, reinstall.

00:10:18.032 --> 00:10:21.465
The icon is there, and
a tap starts the app.

00:10:22.617 --> 00:10:24.481
Mistake two:

00:10:24.601 --> 00:10:28.970
MainActivity forgets to
implement SaveQuantityListener.

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:30.963
It still compiles.

00:10:31.083 --> 00:10:37.301
Tap Set quantity, and it crashes.
Logcat: ClassCastException,

00:10:37.421 --> 00:10:41.717
MainActivity cannot be cast
to SaveQuantityListener.

00:10:42.867 --> 00:10:45.700
The fix: add it to implements.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:50.212
Rerun, type seven,
Save: Quantity seven.

00:10:51.367 --> 00:10:54.440
Mistake three:
commit is forgotten.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.792
No error, no crash.

00:10:56.912 --> 00:10:59.265
The container is just empty.

00:10:59.385 --> 00:11:01.251
The sneaky one.

00:11:02.408 --> 00:11:06.417
Add commit, rebuild, and
HomeFragment is back.

00:11:07.575 --> 00:11:10.552
Mistake four: inflate with true.

00:11:10.672 --> 00:11:12.929
The app crashes on launch.

00:11:13.049 --> 00:11:17.369
Logcat: the specified child
already has a parent.

00:11:17.489 --> 00:11:21.138
With true, the view is
added to the container now,

00:11:21.258 --> 00:11:24.115
and the FragmentManager
adds it again.

00:11:25.242 --> 00:11:27.307
The fix: false.

00:11:27.427 --> 00:11:30.499
Rebuild, rerun, and it works.

00:11:31.658 --> 00:11:33.523
Key takeaways.

00:11:33.643 --> 00:11:35.516
Pause and copy them.

00:11:35.636 --> 00:11:38.348
Fragment: a piece of a screen.

00:11:38.468 --> 00:11:41.493
Inflate with false,
return the view.

00:11:41.613 --> 00:11:45.166
beginTransaction,
replace, commit.

00:11:45.286 --> 00:11:52.244
Dialog: interface, cast in onAttach,
call the listener, dismiss.

00:11:52.364 --> 00:11:55.245
The host implements
it and calls show.

00:11:55.365 --> 00:12:00.502
Manifest: every Activity, MAIN
and LAUNCHER, no fragments.

00:12:00.622 --> 00:12:03.310
Versions in build dot gradle.

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:06.318
Your turn.

